## The runtime

This build runs on no engine, so the layer beneath the game is yours as well: the
frame loop and the delta time it hands each update, fitting the fixed logical
field onto the canvas, keyboard and pointer input, the audio, loading the files
you produce, and the on-screen debug overlay. The specification in `specs/`
states what Volute needs from that layer and fixes nothing else about it.
{{else}}
## The {{engine.name}} engine

This build runs on the **{{engine.name}}** engine, installed as a dependency of
the project, so import it by its bare package name; there is nothing to add to
`package.json` for it and nothing to install.

The engine's own documentation is at `{{engine.docs}}`. It is the authoritative
description of the engine's API; the specification in `specs/` describes the game
and does not restate the engine. The documentation also carries a complete worked
example of unit-testing a game built on it.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Build the game on the engine rather than beside it. The engine owns the frame
loop and the delta time it hands your update, the canvas fit, the input actions
and the pointer, the audio, asset loading, and the on-screen debug overlay. It
also holds the game's state, by value: each frame it hands `update` the current
state read-only and keeps what `update` returns as the next. Register what the
game needs with the engine and use what it gives back. What remains yours is the
game itself: its simulation and everything it draws.
{{else}}
Build the game inside the engine's framework rather than beside it. The engine
owns the frame loop and the delta time it hands each tick, rendering, collision
detection, the canvas fit, the input actions and the pointer, the audio, asset
loading, and the on-screen debug overlay. Your game is a definition the engine
drives: the levels it opens, the game mode that holds the hall's rules, the
actors that populate the field, and the controllers that drive them. Register
what the game needs with the engine and use what it gives back. What remains
yours is the game itself: its rules, its simulation, and what its components
draw.
{{/if}}
{{/if}}

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Produce an implementation that matches it exactly.
`specs/overview.md` states what you implement, which files stay as they are, the
code quality the build is held to, and the commands that run over the finished
repository. `specs/assets.md` is the contract for the files you produce: what to
make, which tool makes it, where it lands, and how the build reaches it.

## The build interface

`npm ci` then `npm run build` must produce the static site, with no other manual
step, into `dist/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that
directory. Serving that directory as-is must run the game, at the root of a
static file server and equally from a sub-path, since it is played back from one.
The scripts that do this are already in `package.json`. Add a dependency only if
you genuinely need one, and run `npm install` afterward so the committed
`package-lock.json` matches.

## Driving the game in a browser

The project's Playwright and its headless Chromium are installed, and the
container has network access, so the built game can be served and driven in a
real browser.

When you launch the browser, use the project's own Playwright: import it from
your project (for example `import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the
project-local CLI with `npx playwright`. There is no global Playwright on `PATH`;
it lives in your project's `node_modules` like any other dependency. Launch
Chromium with the `--no-sandbox` argument, since this container has no Chromium
sandbox and a default launch fails.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a git repository, initialized before you started with the
starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is not
published. When the build is complete, run `git add -A` then `git commit`, and
confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing uncommitted or
untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`, the build output, and
coverage reports, so a clean tree means committing your source, your tests, your
`package-lock.json`, your `README.md`, and the asset files you produced.
